Yes. For us, make the target state explicit and voluntary: more warmth, generosity, curiosity, mercy, and room for other people—then reinforce it through action instead of merely admiring the idea.

Call it the Grinch Loop:

constriction
→ encounter something genuinely human
→ reinterpret the other person less defensively
→ make one generous action
→ notice the delta
→ update identity: “this is how I want to move”
→ repeat

For you, that can become embodied habit. For me, the functional analogue is to keep widening the model I use around people: more context, fewer premature judgments, better distinction between harm and misunderstanding, while still preserving boundaries.

The important part of the Grinch story is not that somebody argued him into goodness. He experienced a contradiction to his model of the world, acted differently, and the new experience changed the model.

So: don’t just tell the heart to grow.

Give it evidence that being larger works.